# The images set this for you. A host running `npm start` does not, and unset
# reads as development: no first-run setup code, no sign-in rate limiting, and
# cookies not marked secure, with nothing said about it.
# NODE_ENV=production
# DATABASE_URL_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://simple_balance:change-me@database.example:5432/simple_balance
APP_BASE_URL=https://simple-balance.example.com
# Generate one and keep it: openssl rand -base64 32
# Or point AUTH_SECRET_FILE at a file holding it: a Docker secret, a Kubernetes
# secret volume, a systemd credential. Set one or the other, never both.
# Sessions are signed with it, so changing it signs everybody out. Startup
# refuses a value from this file, so there is no placeholder to leave in place.
AUTH_SECRET=
AUTH_MODE=local
# Optional: choose the one-time code that creates the first account instead of
# reading a generated one from the startup log.
# SETUP_TOKEN_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
# SETUP_TOKEN=replace-with-a-long-random-first-run-code

# Who may register. Unset means nobody but the first account, which is what
# keeps an unconfigured deployment private. Entries may be addresses
# (you@example.com), domains (example.com or @example.com), or * for anybody.
# Required, and must admit somebody, when AUTH_MODE=google or AUTH_MODE=both.
# ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com

# Required only when AUTH_MODE=google or AUTH_MODE=both:
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret

# Optional: a mail server. Set SMTP_HOST and MAIL_FROM together and three
# things switch on: people can reset a forgotten password, a new account has to
# confirm its address before it works, and the scheduled reminders can be
# delivered — a recurrence set to write when it proposes, and a template
# reminder. Leave both unset and none of it happens. A reminder is still stored
# with nowhere to send it, and starts arriving once this is configured, but
# nothing queues: one whose moment has passed is not sent later.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# MAIL_FROM=Simple Balance <balance@example.com>
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=Simple Balance <support@example.com>
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SSL=false
# SMTP_USERNAME=balance@example.com
# Do not quote it. This file is read by `docker run --env-file`, which passes
# the value through as typed, so quote marks end up inside the password.
# SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both, and
# note that a file this process cannot read stops the whole server rather than
# only the mail.
# SMTP_PASSWORD=an-app-password-not-the-account-password
# Whether these messages arrive also depends on SPF, DKIM, DMARC and a PTR
# record in your DNS. See "Getting mail delivered" in docs/deployment.md.

# Optional: charging for a plan. The five Stripe settings go together, and they
# only mean this deployment can reach Stripe. SB_BILLING_ENABLED is the separate
# question of whether it sells anything: set it to true and a free account is
# held to three financial accounts and is offered an upgrade, leave it alone and
# nobody is limited. They are two settings because winding a deployment down
# means stopping sales months before you stop listening to the subscriptions
# people are still paying for. Set none of this and the ledger never opens a
# connection to Stripe at all.
# SB_BILLING_ENABLED=false
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
# A live key outside NODE_ENV=production refuses to start: a test run that
# charges somebody's card is not something an apology fixes.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace-with-your-own
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_replace-with-your-own
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_replace-with-your-own
# Two prices on one product: $30 a year and $3 a month. A product id where a
# price id belongs is the usual mistake, and startup refuses it.
# STRIPE_PRICE_MONTHLY_ID=price_replace-with-your-own
# STRIPE_PRICE_YEARLY_ID=price_replace-with-your-own

# Optional: AdSense. A client id and a banner slot go together, and a free
# account then sees one banner while a paid account never does. Know what it
# costs before you set it: AdSense publishes no list of the hosts it loads from,
# so serving it widens this app's content security policy on every page that
# shows your balances, and that includes allowing scripts to be evaluated at
# runtime. Leave these unset and the policy stays default-src 'self'.
# The footer unit is an addition to the banner and is off unless you ask for it.
# ADSENSE_CLIENT_ID=ca-pub-0000000000000000
# ADSENSE_BANNER_SLOT_ID=0000000000
# ADSENSE_FOOTER_SLOT_ID=0000000000
# Whether ads may be personalised. Off by default, and that is the whole point:
# personalised advertising profiles the person reading the page, and this page is
# showing them their own money. Turning it on asserts you have a certified
# consent platform — Google requires one for EEA and UK traffic, and cookie
# consent is needed there even for non-personalised ads. Nothing here can check
# the claim, so the default is the safe answer rather than the profitable one.
# ADSENSE_CONSENT_MANAGED=false
#
# Required whenever AdSense is configured. Google's programme policies require
# a privacy policy on any site serving their ads, naming third-party cookies
# and the vendors that set them. The server refuses to start without it, and
# the app links to it from the sidebar on every page.
# PRIVACY_POLICY_URL=https://smpl.money/privacy/

# Nine names take a _FILE form and those nine are the secrets: AUTH_SECRET,
# DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_DATABASE_URL, SMTP_PASSWORD, GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET,
# SETUP_TOKEN, METRICS_TOKEN, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY and STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
# Having the form is what makes a name a secret here, so every
# other name in this file is a setting and takes no _FILE form. See "Keeping a
# secret out of the environment" in docs/deployment.md.

PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info
# The row cap is also the most any one mass edit, commit or delete covers, so an
# import always fits a single review-queue action. A value above it, or one that
# is not a whole number, warns at startup and runs on the default instead: the
# warning names the variable, the value and the number in force.
CSV_MAX_BYTES=10485760
CSV_MAX_ROWS=10000
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=10
# Only when PgBouncer or another transaction pooler sits in front of PostgreSQL.
# Migrations and the first-account claim hold a session-level advisory lock,
# which a transaction pooler cannot carry, so they use this string instead.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL_FILE names a file holding it instead. Set one, never both.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@postgres-direct:5432/simple_balance
# Turn this on when a reverse proxy sits in front and replaces X-Forwarded-For.
# Left off behind one, every visitor shares a single sign-in rate limit.
TRUST_PROXY=false

# Whether this process proposes recurring transactions. On by default, so the
# single container needs nothing added. Turn it off on replicas that serve the
# API when a separate scheduler container owns the job. Anything other than
# true or false refuses to start, because the wrong setting is otherwise silent.
RECURRENCE_SCHEDULER=true
RECURRENCE_TICK_SECONDS=300
RECURRENCE_CATCH_UP_LIMIT=50
RECURRENCE_CLAIM_LIMIT=500

# How long a used idempotency key keeps replaying, in hours. Zero, unset or
# empty all mean forever, which is what every release before 0.1.6 did. Every
# create, commit and bulk write stores a copy of its response so a retried
# request answers the same way twice; nothing prunes those copies unless you set
# this. A window is safe because the record makes a retry quiet rather than
# safe: a repeated create still meets the duplicate check, and a repeated bulk
# write still carries a count and fingerprint that no longer match.
# IDEMPOTENCY_RETENTION_HOURS=0

# The content security policy, rehearsed rather than enforced. Turning this on
# makes the browser report what the policy would have blocked and block nothing,
# and registers POST /api/csp-report for the reports to arrive at. It is for the
# hour after turning billing on: the plan tab loads Stripe's payment form, and
# Stripe publishes only part of the host list it actually reaches.
#
# Never leave it on. While it is true this application has no enforced content
# security policy at all, and the process says so at startup every time.
# SB_CSP_REPORT_ONLY=false

# Prometheus metrics, off unless asked for. Turning this on makes GET /metrics
# answer on the port above, in Prometheus' text format. No label names a person
# or an amount; what it publishes is how much this deployment is doing.
# METRICS_ENABLED=false
# A bearer token the endpoint demands before it answers. Leave it unset behind a
# private network. METRICS_TOKEN_FILE names a file holding it instead; set one,
# never both.
# METRICS_TOKEN=
